<<<md>>>
# Point Spread Function Analysis

**Part:** I — Image quality & artifacts · **Reading time:** ~75 min · **Pass 1 overview:** [Contrast and Image Quality](../pass1_big_picture/P1.4_Contrast_and_Image_Quality.ipynb), [Speed, Artifacts and Safety](../pass1_big_picture/P1.5_Speed_Artifacts_and_Safety.ipynb) · **Refreshers you may want:** [Continuous FT and DFT](../refreshers/RM3_Continuous_FT_and_DFT.ipynb), [Fourier properties (shift, convolution theorem)](../refreshers/RM4_Fourier_Properties.ipynb), [Fourier pairs and sampling](../refreshers/RM5_Fourier_Pairs_and_Sampling.ipynb), [2D Fourier and images](../refreshers/RM6_2D_Fourier_and_Images.ipynb)

An MRI scanner never delivers the "true" object $m(\vec r)$. It delivers a version of it that has been **blurred, shifted, ringed and replicated** by the way the data were acquired. The **point spread function (PSF)** — the image the scanner would produce of a single, infinitely small point — captures *all* of these effects in one function. In this chapter we show how to use the PSF together with the MRI signal equation to predict, quantitatively, what finite sampling, relaxation during the readout, off-resonance and chemical shift do to the image. The same toolbox explains most artifacts in the next chapter, [Artifacts](I2_Artifacts.ipynb).

<<<md>>>
## Learning goals

1. Describe how images are formed
    * What is the impact of relaxation and off-resonance/chemical shift on the signal
1. Understand the constraints and trade-offs in MRI
    * How do relaxation and off-resonance/chemical shift limit the readout duration
1. Manipulate MRI sequence parameters to improve performance
    * How does the readout duration affect artifacts due to relaxation and off-resonance/chemical shift
1. Identify artifacts and how to mitigate them
    * What is the effect of relaxation and off-resonance/chemical shift during the readout on the resulting images

New in this adaptation:

5. Define the PSF, explain **linear shift-invariant (LSI)** systems and **convolution** from scratch.
6. Derive the PSF of finite, discrete k-space sampling (the **Dirichlet kernel** ≈ sinc) and explain why sampling creates **replicas** (aliasing).
7. Derive the blur from $T_2$/$T_2^*$ decay during the readout (a Lorentzian-like PSF) and relate it to blurring in fast spin echo (FSE) and echo-planar imaging (EPI).
8. Derive the image shift from off-resonance: **shift = Δf / (bandwidth per pixel)** pixels.
9. Quantify the trade-offs of **windowing/apodization** (resolution vs. sidelobes vs. SNR).

